1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,698
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Logging into AICR with Claude. How to let

2
00:00:04,718 --> 00:00:10,091
Claude Code log into the AICR cluster for you and run commands there, while your key

3
00:00:10,111 --> 00:00:12,450
and your passphrase never leave your hands.

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:17,826
Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. To let Claude log into AICR,

5
00:00:17,846 --> 00:00:23,151
the shared GPU cluster, you do not hand it your password. AICR has no password. You

6
00:00:23,171 --> 00:00:26,480
unlock a key once, yourself, and Claude never sees it.

7
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:31,229
Six terms. A private key: a secret file that proves who you are. A certificate: a

8
00:00:31,249 --> 00:00:35,341
signed note from AICR saying that key is yours. A passphrase: the password that

9
00:00:35,361 --> 00:00:39,747
locks the key file. The ssh-agent: a helper that holds your unlocked key in memory.

10
00:00:39,767 --> 00:00:44,153
BatchMode: a setting that makes ssh refuse to ask any question. And a login node:

11
00:00:44,173 --> 00:00:47,090
the shared computer you land on when you log in.

12
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:52,462
AICR has no password. At the front door it checks two things. A private key, a

13
00:00:52,482 --> 00:00:57,119
secret file that only you hold. And a certificate, a note that AICR's own

14
00:00:57,139 --> 00:00:59,780
certificate authority signed, saying that key is yours.

15
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:04,853
To get in at all, you need an account. A faculty member's proposal opens a project,

16
00:01:04,873 --> 00:01:09,563
and your institution creates your user. Your AICR user name is your campus name with

17
00:01:09,583 --> 00:01:13,330
the institution on the end, like j smith underscore n e u.

18
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:19,625
The key files come from one place: the AICR OnDemand portal, at o o d dot aicr dot

19
00:01:19,645 --> 00:01:24,483
AI. You sign in there with your institutional credentials. That step is yours, not

20
00:01:24,503 --> 00:01:24,830
Claude's.

21
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:29,576
Download the folder called aicr keys. Inside are four files. Your private key,

22
00:01:29,596 --> 00:01:33,910
locked with a passphrase. Its public half. Your certificate, which is the

23
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:36,800
credential. And a file holding your first passphrase.

24
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:41,561
Signing in is the human step. So are putting the files in place and choosing a new

25
00:01:41,581 --> 00:01:45,441
passphrase. Do not give Claude your institutional password, and do not ask it to

26
00:01:45,461 --> 00:01:46,550
sign in for you.

27
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:51,659
Put the files in your dot ssh folder, set their permissions, and swap the first

28
00:01:51,679 --> 00:01:56,381
passphrase for one only you know. Windows is the same, in PowerShell. Then delete

29
00:01:56,401 --> 00:01:57,730
the downloaded passphrase file.

30
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:02,632
Check the certificate with ssh-keygen dash L. Valid is a date range, or forever.

31
00:02:02,652 --> 00:02:08,177
Principals must be your AICR user name. When it nears its end, renew it in the

32
00:02:08,197 --> 00:02:08,870
OnDemand portal.

33
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:13,950
Now the problem. Claude Code runs commands with no keyboard attached. If ssh stops

34
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:18,980
to ask for the passphrase, nobody can answer, and the command hangs or fails.

35
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:24,534
The fix is not to give Claude the passphrase. The fix is to unlock the key before

36
00:02:24,554 --> 00:02:28,744
Claude arrives, once, in your own terminal, into a helper called ssh-agent. The

37
00:02:28,764 --> 00:02:31,010
agent holds the unlocked key in memory.

38
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:36,181
From then on, when ssh needs the key, it asks the agent, and the agent answers. The

39
00:02:36,201 --> 00:02:40,399
key never leaves the agent. Claude does the work. The secret never passes through

40
00:02:40,419 --> 00:02:40,700
it.

41
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:46,632
Add one block to your ssh config. Host aicr gives the cluster a short name. Then its

42
00:02:46,652 --> 00:02:51,130
host name, your AICR user name, your key file, and your certificate file.

43
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:55,497
Identities only offers just this key. Server alive interval stops idle connections

44
00:02:55,517 --> 00:03:01,253
dropping. Add keys to agent loads the key into the agent on first use. Use keychain

45
00:03:01,273 --> 00:03:05,570
is for macOS only, and ignore unknown makes other systems skip it.

46
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:11,175
On a Mac, unlock it with S S H add and the apple keychain flag, so the passphrase

47
00:03:11,195 --> 00:03:15,804
survives restarts. On Linux, start an agent, add the key, and start Claude Code from

48
00:03:15,824 --> 00:03:16,730
that same terminal.

49
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:21,811
On Windows, open PowerShell as administrator, once. Set the agent service to start

50
00:03:21,831 --> 00:03:24,520
automatically, start it, and add the key.

51
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:28,735
Each of these asks for your passphrase, in your own terminal. Type it there, never

52
00:03:28,755 --> 00:03:34,011
in a prompt to Claude. Then ask the agent what it holds. One line ending in E D two

53
00:03:34,031 --> 00:03:36,510
five five one nine means the key is loaded.

54
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:42,642
Test it by hand first. Run ssh aicr hostname. You should get login dot aicr dot AI.

55
00:03:42,662 --> 00:03:47,651
The very first connection asks you to confirm the host key. Type yes yourself,

56
00:03:47,671 --> 00:03:49,440
because Claude cannot answer that.

57
00:03:49,510 --> 00:03:54,104
Then test it the way Claude connects: with BatchMode on. BatchMode makes ssh fail at

58
00:03:54,124 --> 00:03:58,717
once instead of asking anything. If it prints your user name and the host, Claude

59
00:03:58,737 --> 00:03:59,640
can log in.

60
00:03:59,710 --> 00:04:04,742
Now a real run. Claude Code ran one ssh command with BatchMode on. Before this call,

61
00:04:04,762 --> 00:04:10,110
the agent held no keys. The command asks for the user name, the host, and the date.

62
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:14,541
The cluster answered with its seven partitions, and an empty job queue, because

63
00:04:14,561 --> 00:04:19,260
nothing was running. The last line is the home directory, named after the user.

64
00:04:19,330 --> 00:04:23,549
Now the test that matters. When you reboot, the agent forgets everything. So empty

65
00:04:23,569 --> 00:04:26,880
it on purpose, and see whether Claude can still get in.

66
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:32,410
Ask the agent what it holds: one key. Empty it with S S H add dash capital D. Ask

67
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:36,160
again: nothing. The agent is empty, just as it is after a reboot.

68
00:04:36,230 --> 00:04:40,907
Now log in the way Claude does, with BatchMode on and an empty agent. It still

69
00:04:40,927 --> 00:04:45,310
works. No prompt, no passphrase typed. And ask the agent again: the key is back.

70
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:50,210
On a Mac, that is the Keychain at work. Add keys to agent asks it for the

71
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:54,204
passphrase, and the key goes back into the agent. Nothing was typed, and nothing

72
00:04:54,224 --> 00:04:55,060
passed to Claude.

73
00:04:55,130 --> 00:04:59,720
On Linux and Windows there is no Keychain to reload the key. So this same test fails

74
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:05,414
until you run S S H add again. That is expected. It tells you to run S S H add at

75
00:05:05,434 --> 00:05:06,770
the start of each session.

76
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:12,906
How did it get in? Ask ssh to show its steps. It found one key in the agent, and

77
00:05:12,926 --> 00:05:16,749
offered the certificate. The server accepted it, and ssh reports it authenticated

78
00:05:16,769 --> 00:05:17,710
with public key.

79
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:22,456
When you ask, Claude runs one ssh command with BatchMode on, followed by your

80
00:05:22,476 --> 00:05:27,151
read-only commands. Claude Code asks you to approve it. On a shared system, approve

81
00:05:27,171 --> 00:05:30,170
each ssh command, at least the first few times.

82
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:35,389
Rule one. Read only first. Look before you change anything: s info, s queue for your

83
00:05:35,409 --> 00:05:38,620
own jobs, s acct, job stats, ls, and module avail.

84
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:43,652
Rule two. Never compute on a login node. Login nodes are shared, capped at four CPU

85
00:05:43,672 --> 00:05:48,012
cores and eight gigabytes of memory per user. Heavy work goes through Slurm, the

86
00:05:48,032 --> 00:05:52,060
scheduler: s batch, or s alloc and s run for a live session.

87
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:57,727
Rule three. Ask before s batch, s cancel, r m, or anything that writes outside your

88
00:05:57,747 --> 00:06:01,940
own project. Jobs use shared GPUs, and they count against your project.

89
00:06:02,010 --> 00:06:06,692
Rule four. Never print, copy, or upload the private key. Not cat, not s c p, not

90
00:06:06,712 --> 00:06:11,670
into the chat. Claude does not need to read the key to use it. The agent does that.

91
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:16,588
Rule five. Never put the passphrase in a prompt, a file, or a command. If a command

92
00:06:16,608 --> 00:06:20,310
asks for it, stop, and unlock the key yourself, in your own terminal.

93
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:25,445
Rule six. Use your own account only. The certificate names you, so do not use it to

94
00:06:25,465 --> 00:06:26,940
run work for anyone else.

95
00:06:27,010 --> 00:06:31,945
And rule seven. Mind where files live. Home is small and recoverable. Scratch is

96
00:06:31,965 --> 00:06:36,900
large, and it is purged. Check AICR's file system page before you move data.

97
00:06:36,970 --> 00:06:41,399
The error you will see most is permission denied, public key. It usually means one

98
00:06:41,419 --> 00:06:46,441
of three things. The key is not unlocked, by far the most common after a reboot. Or

99
00:06:46,461 --> 00:06:50,000
the certificate is expired or missing. Or the key's permissions are wrong.

100
00:06:50,070 --> 00:06:54,332
Here is the trap. An empty agent looks exactly like an expired certificate. The

101
00:06:54,352 --> 00:07:00,144
error is the same. So check the agent with S S H add dash l before you blame the

102
00:07:00,164 --> 00:07:00,450
certificate.

103
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:05,704
Then, if the agent holds the key, check the certificate: is it still valid? If it is

104
00:07:05,724 --> 00:07:09,990
not, renew it in OnDemand. And set the private key's permissions to six hundred.

105
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:15,042
Other messages have short fixes. A command that hangs or wants a passphrase: unlock

106
00:07:15,062 --> 00:07:19,686
the key yourself. Host key verification failed: remove the old entry with ssh-keygen

107
00:07:19,706 --> 00:07:24,330
dash R, then connect once yourself. A new host key prompt: type yes.

108
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:28,470
Bad configuration option: add ignore unknown above the keychain line. Connection

109
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:33,675
timed out: try another network, because AICR needs no VPN. Drops when idle: server

110
00:07:33,695 --> 00:07:39,996
alive interval sixty. Empty after a restart: S S H add again, on a Mac with the

111
00:07:40,016 --> 00:07:40,740
keychain flag.

112
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:45,227
Let's recap with Claude. AICR checks a key and a certificate, not a password. Claude

113
00:07:45,247 --> 00:07:49,664
cannot type a passphrase, so you unlock the key once into the ssh-agent. Test with

114
00:07:49,684 --> 00:07:53,806
BatchMode: even an emptied agent logged in on a Mac. Claude runs read-only commands,

115
00:07:53,826 --> 00:07:58,243
and asks before it changes anything. The key and the passphrase never go into a

116
00:07:58,263 --> 00:08:02,384
prompt. And when you see permission denied, check the agent before you blame the

117
00:08:02,404 --> 00:08:02,680
certificate.

118
00:08:02,750 --> 00:08:07,664
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Log into the AICR cluster with ssh aicr and run

119
00:08:07,684 --> 00:08:11,673
read-only commands only. Run: whoami, hostname, pwd, s info with its output format

120
00:08:11,693 --> 00:08:16,915
set to percent fourteen P, percent eleven l, percent six D, and s queue for my own

121
00:08:16,935 --> 00:08:21,540
user. Tell me the user name, the host, and which partitions exist. Do not submit,

122
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:26,166
cancel or change anything, and do not run computation on the login node. Then check

123
00:08:26,186 --> 00:08:30,483
two things yourself. Did Claude run only read-only commands? And did it ever ask

124
00:08:30,503 --> 00:08:32,950
for, or print, your passphrase or your key?

125
00:08:33,020 --> 00:08:37,080
Logging into AICR with Claude. At Nik Bear Brown.
